6. To follow the course of by observation the remains or vestiges; ascertain the positi course, contour, etc., of by noting and follo ing the traces that exist.

{{smaller block|You may ''trace'' out the Aqueduct all along by the maining fragments of it.
{{right|''Maundrell'', Aleppo to Jerusalem, p.}}}}

{{smaller block|On the seventeenth we took another view of the val Jehosaphat. And on the twentieth ''traced'' the old w to the north, and reviewed the places that way.
{{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, II. i.}}}}

{{smaller block|The sepulchres of Rome have as yet been far too c lessly examined to enable us to ''trace'' all the steps which the transformation took place.
{{right|''J. Fergusson'', Hist. Arch., I.}}}}

7. To observe traces or vestiges of; disco visible evidences or proofs of.

{{smaller block|You may ''trace'' the deluge quite round the globe.
{{right|''T. Burnet'', Theory of the Earth,}}}}

{{smaller block|In his frank eyes she did not fail to ''trace''
A trouble like unto a growing hate,
That, yet unknown to him, her love did wait.
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, III.}}}}

8. To follow step by step: as, to ''trace'' the velopment of a plot: often with ''up'', ''back'', ''ou''

{{smaller block|He ''traced up'' his descent on both sides for several erations.
{{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No.}}}}

{{smaller block|There is no prosperity, trade, art, city, or great m rial wealth of any kind, but if you ''trace'' it home you find it rooted in a thought of some individual man.
{{right|''Emerson'', Succ}}}}

