II. ''intrans.'' 1. To move; go; march; make one's way; travel.

{{smaller block|Our present worldes lyves space
Nis but a maner deth, what weye we ''trace''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parliament of Fowls, l.}}}}

{{smaller block|Not wont on foot with heavy armes to ''trace''.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., VI. iii.}}}}

{{smaller block|He would now be up every morning by break of ''tracing'' and walking to and fro in the valley.
{{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim's Progress}}}}

2†. To step; pace; dance.

{{smaller block|For Coridon could daunce, and trimly ''trace''.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., VI. ix.}}}}

'''trace<sup>1</sup>''' (trās), ''n.'' [< ME. ''trace, traas,'' < OF. ''trace'', F. ''trace'' = Pr. ''trassa, tras'' = Sp. ''traza'' = Pg. ''traço'' = It. ''traccia'', an outline, track, trace; from the verb.] 1. The track left by a person or animal walking or running over the ground or other surface, as snow or the like; footprints; the track, trail, or rut left by something which is drawn along, as a cart; the marks which indicate the course pursued by any moving thing.

{{smaller block|These as a line their long dimension drew,
Streaking the ground with sinuous ''trace''.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., vii.}}}}

