{{smaller block|Hans Van Ripper now began to feel some uneasi about the fate of poor Ichabod and his saddle. An quiry was set on foot, and after diligent investigation came upon his ''traces''. {{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p.}}}}

2†. Hence, a track or path; a way.

{{smaller block|As traytoures on-trewe the sall teche them a ''trac''
{{right|''York Plays'', p.}}}}

{{smaller block|Let reason thee rule, and not will thee leade
To folowe thy fansie, A wronge ''trace'' to treade.
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p.}}}}

{{smaller block|Alexis, let us rest here, if the place
Be private, and out of the common ''trace''
Of every shepherd.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Faithful Shepherdess, i}}}}

3. A token, indication, or sign of someth that has passed over or away; a mark, imp sion, or visible evidence of something that occurred or existed; a vestige.

{{smaller block|The shady empire shall retain no ''trace''
Of war or blood but in the sylvan chase.
{{right|''Pope'', Windsor Forest, l.}}}}

{{smaller block|Such dreams of baseless good
Oft come and go, in crowds or solitude,
And leave no ''trace''. ''Shelley'', Julian and Mad}}

{{smaller block|On the worn features of the weariest face
Some youthful memory leaves its hidden ''trace''.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', The Old Pl}}}}

4. A small quantity; an insignificant pro tion: as, tetradymite or telluride of bism usually contains ''traces'' of selenium.

{{smaller block|At one time our thoughts are distorted by the pas running through them; and at another time it is diff to detect in them a ''trace'' of liking or disliking.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Prin. of Sociol., §}}}}

